<?php
/**
 * Example configuration for the REST API's security collaborators.
 *
 * Copy to config/restapi_inc.php and edit. Nothing here is read from this file
 * unless it exists there — the path the application looks at is
 * config/restapi_inc.php in the docroot, which is created per installation and
 * is deliberately not kept in the repository.
 *
 * WHY A FILE AND NOT ONLY THE ENVIRONMENT. Both constants below can be set as
 * environment variables (CHISIMBA_JWKS_KEY_PATH, CHISIMBA_DENYLIST_REDIS), but
 * an environment variable cannot be changed without recreating the container. A
 * setting that can only be changed by rebuilding is a setting that gets left
 * wrong, and leaving the key path wrong means the service cannot start at all.
 * The environment is read first and this file second; neither is a fallback for
 * the other, because a value that is right in one and wrong in the other does
 * something worse than either alone — see the notes on each.
 *
 * @package core_modules
 * @subpackage restapi
 */

/**
 * Where the private key that signs tokens is kept.
 *
 * Generated on first use and never replaced, so changing this path publishes a
 * different key and invalidates every token already issued. That is a deliberate
 * event, not something to do to fix a path.
 *
 * Point it at a mounted secret outside the web root, in an existing private
 * directory. There is no source-tree fallback. When upgrading, retain the
 * existing key bytes so issued tokens remain valid.
 *
 * Neither source is a fallback for the other: a path that is right in the
 * environment and wrong here silently publishes a *different* key on every
 * start, which looks like an ordinary redeploy while invalidating every token in
 * circulation.
 */
if (!defined('KEWL_JWKS_KEY_PATH')) {
    define('KEWL_JWKS_KEY_PATH', '/var/data/restapi/jwks_private.pem');
}

/**
 * The Redis the field services read their revocation denylist from.
 *
 * No default, deliberately. Defaulting to localhost would look like a working
 * revocation on installations where nothing was being published, which is
 * precisely the failure the denylist exists to prevent. With this unset and
 * CHISIMBA_DENYLIST_REDIS unset, a sign-in is refused rather than issued a token
 * nobody can revoke.
 *
 * format: host:port
 */
if (!defined('KEWL_DENYLIST_REDIS')) {
    define('KEWL_DENYLIST_REDIS', 'localhost:6379');
}

/**
 * Where the field guiding service is, as this installation reaches it.
 *
 * This is the address the REST API proxies to: the species, outing and
 * competency catalogues, and the sync endpoints. It has a default
 * (http://field-service:8080, the compose service name on the shared network)
 * because those routes are the only ones that answer from another container,
 * and an unconfigured installation should still serve its catalogue rather
 * than 503 on every species request.
 *
 * Set CHISIMBA_FIELD_SERVICE_URL in the environment, or here, when the service
 * is reached any other way: a different compose project name, a service mesh, a
 * host-published port. Include the scheme and the port, with no trailing slash
 * and no path — the routes append /api/v1/species and /api/v1/sync/... themselves.
 *
 * format: scheme://host:port
 */
if (!defined('KEWL_FIELD_SERVICE_URL')) {
    define('KEWL_FIELD_SERVICE_URL', 'http://field-service:8080');
}